    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d70">

                <pb facs="0150" n="116" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v15p1/pg116"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">795.00/3–2452: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Joint Chiefs of Staff to the Commander in Chief
                        Far East (Ridgway)</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d70-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top
                            secret<lb/>operational immediate</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-03-22T13:15:00-05:00">March 22, 1952—1:15
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>JCS 904385. From JCS. Re para 8 your C 65650.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d70-f2"/></p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>As indicated in JCS 904101<anchor n="3" xml:id="d70-f3"/> we have no
                        objection to your making further efforts to obtain agreement with Communist
                        on an acceptable basis for POW exchange. Communist statements in staff ofcr
                        mtgs of past 2 days coupled with impressions contained in your CX
                            65592<anchor n="4" xml:id="d70-f4"/> would seem to indicate definite
                        possibilities in this regard. We feel that Communist position should be
                        explored as carefully and thoroughly <pb facs="0151" n="117" xml:id="pg117"
                        /> as possible. We suggest that this might best be accomplished, at least
                        initially, through a more informal and flexible approach than that embodied
                        in para 6 CX 65424.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d70-f5"/> In this connection we
                        consider private mtgs would offer definite advantages and should be used if
                        practicable. In any event we suggest that timing of issuance of formal
                        specific proposal contained in CX 65424, which Communists might consider
                        must be either accepted or rejected, might well be held in abeyance pending
                        further informal exploratory negots. Such exploratory negots might indicate
                        desirability of modifying proposed formal proposal or might even remove need
                        for such proposal. Therefore, final sentence of para 4A JCS 904101 should
                        not be interpreted as approval for presentation of proposal along lines set
                        forth in para 6 CX 65424 until from further exploration of Communist
                        position it is clear to you that presentation of such proposal would offer
                        definite possibilities for settlement of agenda item 4 without dangers set
                        forth in para 2 JCS 904101.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Pending outcome of above approach, final decision on your other
                        recommendations outstanding as well as approach outlined in JCS
                            903687<anchor n="6" xml:id="d70-f6"/> is withheld.</item>
                </list>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
